{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's cross-city modulator is empirically backwards. It claims arid cities have a larger nocturnal UHI because they have less vegetation, while humid/irrigated cities have a smaller gap. But arid cities (e.g., Phoenix) typically have a NEGATIVE or very small nighttime UHI in summer because irrigated vegetation and anthropogenic moisture drive strong latent heat flux, whereas humid cities have a LARGER nighttime UHI because evapotranspiration is already atmospherically limited, so urban-rural latent-heat differences collapse and other factors dominate. The target thus inverts the observed arid-vs-humid relationship.","counterexample":["A desert city with extensive municipal irrigation can show a cooler nocturnal urban core than its surrounding desert (negative nocturnal UHI), contradicting the claim that arid cities have a larger gap.","A humid city with abundant regional vegetation and moisture can show a larger nocturnal UHI than a heavily irrigated arid city, contradicting the claim that more vegetation/irrigation always yields a smaller gap."],"target":"8e0cb8116d15383c59ad6b239a1013369db3e4f5216b2a52206a40f6d6b6b08e"}]}